   "text": {
    "L1": "An electric cell has a positive metal cap and a negative flat base.",
    "L2": "To light a bulb, connect both cell terminals through the bulb's two terminals.",
    "L3": "This makes one complete, unbroken path called a closed circuit.",
    "L4": "The filament is the thin coiled wire inside the bulb.",
    "L5": "Current flows one way around the whole loop, and the same current flows all the way round.",
    "L6": "So the bulb does not use up current; the cell's stored energy is used over time.",
    "L7": "If there is a gap, a loose contact, or a broken filament, the circuit is open and the bulb stays dark.",
    "L8": "Remember: use torch cells for experiments, never a wall socket."
   },
